# Lombok for boilerplate

In BNPL backend modules, **always use Lombok** for boilerplate instead of hand-written getters, setters, constructors, `equals`/`hashCode`, or `toString`.

## Required patterns

- Entities / mutable domain: `@Getter` `@Setter` `@NoArgsConstructor` (JPA needs a no-args ctor). Prefer these over `@Data` on JPA entities.
- Prefer `@EqualsAndHashCode(onlyExplicitlyIncluded = true)` + `@EqualsAndHashCode.Include` on the id when equality is needed; do not use `@Data` / full-field `@EqualsAndHashCode` on entities.
- DTOs / simple value carriers: prefer Java `record` when immutable fits; otherwise Lombok (`@Value`, `@Builder`, etc.).
- Logging: use `@Slf4j` instead of manual `LoggerFactory` loggers.
- Keep Lombok on the classpath (`org.projectlombok:lombok`, optional). Do not remove it.

## Examples

```java
// ❌ BAD — hand-written accessors
public class Organization {
  private UUID id;
  public UUID getId() { return id; }
  public void setId(UUID id) { this.id = id; }
}

// ✅ GOOD
@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
@Entity
public class Organization {
  private UUID id;
}
```

```java
// ❌ BAD on JPA entity
@Data
@Entity
public class Organization { ... }

// ✅ GOOD on JPA entity
@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
@Entity
public class Organization { ... }
```
